From: PowOS <mats@powback.com>
Subject: [PATCH] backends/anland: a CPU copy when EGL can't import the consumer's buffers

No root, no GPU (the emulator, a GPU-less phone: llvmpipe, no /dev/kgsl-3d0): Mesa's swrast can't make an EGLImage of
the consumer's gralloc buffers ("Error creating EGLImageKHR: EGL_BAD_ACCESS" -> "failed to import dmabuf 0 as
texture"), so nothing was ever drawn into them and every Linux window (systemsettings) showed blank.

When importDmaBufAsTexture fails, importConsumerBuffer now maps the buffer (mmap of its fd, MAP_SHARED), renders into a
plain offscreen texture with the same content transform, and copyToConsumer reads each frame back into the mapping with
glReadPixels (the damaged rows' band; DMA_BUF_IOCTL_SYNC around it for a dma-buf). The copy is synchronous, so the
render fence is -1 ("ready now"). Called from AnlandEglLayer::doEndFrame (the desktop: the buffer's accumulated damage)
and AnlandWindowTask::render (a task: the whole buffer, it redraws it whole). When the import succeeds (every phone with
a GPU) nothing changes: no mapping is made and the fence path is the same as before.

A buffer the consumer can't share at all (the emulator: ranchu's host-memory gralloc, mmap EPERM) comes as a memfd CPU
shadow (native_consumer.c make_shadow), recognised by F_GET_SEALS and copied the same way; a buffer with no stride (the
emulator reports 0) is taken as tight rows.

docs/research/nonroot-parity.md (Linux windows blank on GPU-less devices) has the plan and the next fallback (memfd
buffers the consumer copies in) for a gralloc whose buffers can't be mapped.
---
